@* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   A markdown field that READS as rendered content and EDITS as raw source (#357).

   The task Description was the last markdown-bearing field in the app that never rendered:
   both surfaces bound it straight into a <textarea>, so the field was permanently in EDIT
   mode and every mirrored task showed `<!-- kanbanflow-sync -->` and `**Issue:**` to the user
   as literal text. The component did not have a rendering bug — it had no READ STATE. This
   adds the missing state, Azure-DevOps style: read renders, one click (or Enter/Space) swaps
   in the raw source with the caret already in it. The detail panel commits and returns to read
   on blur; the edit modal keeps editing until its own Save/Cancel action closes the modal.

   ONE COMPONENT, USED TWICE — TaskDetailPanel (the card-click surface) and EditItemModal
   (the outline's Edit Task). Do not fork it: a second copy is exactly how this codebase ended
   up rendering two different clocks (#306) and is why ActivityTimeline carries the same
   warning. The two call sites differ only in what they do with OnCommit — the panel saves on
   blur through ITaskService, the modal defers to its own Save Changes button — never in how
   the field looks or behaves.

   RENDERING GOES THROUGH MarkdownContent, i.e. Markdig + the Ganss.Xss allow-list in
   Services/MarkdownRenderer.cs. No second renderer, no second sanitiser, no WYSIWYG: storage
   stays markdown text. That matters more here than on the other seven render sites, because a
   task description is generated from a GITHUB ISSUE BODY by scripts/portfolio-sync.mjs — so
   anyone who can open an issue on a synced repo authors bytes that this component turns into
   live DOM. Those bytes were previously inert only because they never left a <textarea>.
   The renderer's two known allow-list defects (#340 global attribute list, #341 class/id
   stripped) are INHERITED here, not fixed and not worsened: this adds one more field to a
   pipeline that already serves seven.

   SYNC MARKERS ARE PROTOCOL, NOT CONTENT (the rule stated on #336). The
   `<!-- kanbanflow-sync --> … <!-- /kanbanflow-sync -->` block is the durable repo↔board
   link: the bytes MUST survive round-trip, and no surface may show them to a human. Both
   halves fall out of this design rather than needing code:
     • invisible — the sanitiser drops HTML comments, so they never reach the DOM. That is
       ASSERTED, not assumed: TaskDescriptionRenderingTests goes red if a renderer
       or dependency change ever starts keeping comments.
     • durable — the textarea shows the RAW source, markers included, so an edit round-trips
       them byte-for-byte. Stripping at the STORAGE boundary would have destroyed the link;
       stripping happens at the RENDER boundary only, which is what #336 asks for.

   NO preventDefault ON THE READ ELEMENT'S KEYDOWN, deliberately. Space scrolls a focused
   non-input element, and the obvious fix — `@@onkeydown:preventDefault` — is evaluated at
   RENDER time in Blazor, so it cannot be scoped to one key: it would swallow Tab as well and
   turn the read element into a keyboard trap. A one-frame scroll is a far smaller defect than
   an unreachable panel, and the textarea is focused with preventScroll immediately after. This
   is also exactly what the existing task-name toggle in TaskDetailPanel does.
   ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ *@

@inject IJSRuntime JS

@if (editing)
{
    <label class="form-label small text-muted mb-1" for="@ElementId">@Label</label>
    <textarea id="@ElementId"
              @ref="editorRef"
              class="form-control form-control-sm"
              rows="@Rows"
              placeholder="@Placeholder"
              data-testid="@editTestId"
              value="@Value"
              @oninput="HandleInputAsync"
              @onblur="HandleBlurAsync"></textarea>
}
else
{
    @* Not a <label for>: in read mode the control is a div, which `for` cannot target, and a
       label pointing at an id that is not in the DOM is worse than no association at all. The
       read element carries its own accessible name instead. *@
    <span class="form-label small text-muted mb-1 d-block">@Label</span>

    @* tabindex="0" + aria-label makes the surface reachable and self-describing for keyboard
       users (Enter/Space handled by HandleReadKeyDown below). role="button" is intentionally
       omitted: ARIA forbids interactive descendants inside that role, and a rendered description
       can contain links. Link clicks are intercepted in JS (KanbanFlow.initMarkdownEditable,
       called from OnAfterRenderAsync) so they navigate normally without triggering BeginEdit. *@
    <div class="markdown-editable-read"
         tabindex="0"
         title="Click to edit"
         aria-label="@readAriaLabel"
         data-testid="@readTestId"
         style="@rowsStyle"
         @ref="readRef"
         @onclick="BeginEdit"
         @onkeydown="HandleReadKeyDown">
        <MarkdownContent Markdown="@Value" CssClass="small" EmptyText="@Placeholder" />
    </div>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>Raw markdown source. Two-way bound: use <c>@@bind-Value</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Value { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public EventCallback<string?> ValueChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Raised on blur, AFTER <see cref="ValueChanged"/> has delivered the final keystroke.
    /// Wire it to persist immediately (TaskDetailPanel); leave it unset to defer to an explicit
    /// save (EditItemModal). Unset is a real mode, not an oversight — it is checked via
    /// <c>HasDelegate</c>.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnCommit { get; set; }

    /// <summary>DOM id of the textarea, so the edit-mode label can target it.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string ElementId { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>Prefix for the two data-testid hooks: <c>{TestId}-read</c> / <c>{TestId}-edit</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string TestId { get; set; } = default!;

    [Parameter] public string Label { get; set; } = "Description";

    /// <summary>Shown in the textarea when empty AND as the read-mode empty state, so the two modes agree.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string Placeholder { get; set; } = "Add a description...";

    [Parameter] public int Rows { get; set; } = 4;

    /// <summary>
    /// Change this to force the field back to READ mode. A fresh open, or a switch to a
    /// different item, must not inherit the previous one's edit state — and the reset cannot
    /// simply run on every OnParametersSet, because the parent re-renders on EVERY keystroke
    /// (ValueChanged), which would eject the user mid-word.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public object? ResetToken { get; set; }

    private bool editing;
    private bool focusPending;
    private ElementReference editorRef;
    private ElementReference readRef;
    private object? lastResetToken;
    private bool resetTokenSeen;

    // Resolved once per parameter change, never in the render path. Computed properties re-run
    // on EVERY render, and this component re-renders on every keystroke while editing — the same
    // trap EditItemModal's RefreshGitHubLinks comment already documents.
    private string readTestId = string.Empty;
    private string editTestId = string.Empty;
    private string readAriaLabel = string.Empty;
    private string rowsStyle = string.Empty;

    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        if (!resetTokenSeen || !Equals(ResetToken, lastResetToken))
        {
            resetTokenSeen = true;
            lastResetToken = ResetToken;
            editing = false;
            focusPending = false;
        }

        readTestId = $"{TestId}-read";
        editTestId = $"{TestId}-edit";
        readAriaLabel = $"Edit {Label}";
        // Drives .markdown-editable-read's min-height so switching modes does not make the
        // surrounding panel jump: the read box reserves the same height the textarea occupies.
        rowsStyle = $"--kf-editable-rows: {Rows};";
    }

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!editing)
        {
            // After each read-mode render, wire up link-click interception so rendered <a>
            // elements do not bubble their clicks to the wrapper's BeginEdit handler.
            try { await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("KanbanFlow.initMarkdownEditable", readRef); }
            catch (JSDisconnectedException) { }
            catch (ObjectDisposedException) { }
            catch (InvalidOperationException) { }
            return;
        }

        // One click, not two. Without this the click lands on a div, the textarea appears
        // unfocused, and the user has to click again — which is the roadblock #357 forbids.
        if (!focusPending) return;
        focusPending = false;

        try
        {
            await editorRef.FocusAsync(preventScroll: true);
        }
        catch (JSDisconnectedException) { }
        catch (ObjectDisposedException) { }
        catch (InvalidOperationException) { }
    }

    private void BeginEdit()
    {
        if (editing) return;
        editing = true;
        focusPending = true;
    }

    private void HandleReadKeyDown(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        // Same key set as TaskDetailPanel's name toggle — keep the two in step.
        if (e.Key is "Enter" or " " or "Spacebar")
        {
            BeginEdit();
        }
    }

    private Task HandleInputAsync(ChangeEventArgs e)
    {
        // Equivalent to @bind:event="oninput", which is what TaskDetailPanel used before #357:
        // the parent holds the buffer, so blur (and the modal's Save button) always sees the
        // latest keystroke without a change event.
        Value = e.Value?.ToString();
        return ValueChanged.InvokeAsync(Value);
    }

    private async Task HandleBlurAsync()
    {
        // An unset callback is the modal's explicit-save mode. Keep the textarea alive across
        // focus changes so clicking another field or the Save button does not unexpectedly turn
        // the description back into read mode before the modal closes (#357 plan step 5).
        if (!OnCommit.HasDelegate) return;

        // Only return to read after the commit completes. If a future save path throws, leaving
        // the editor open preserves the user's buffer and gives them a chance to retry.
        await OnCommit.InvokeAsync();
        editing = false;
    }
}
